L3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 1 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 28 Oct 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Prosperity before Collapse: How Far Can Off-Policy RL Reach with Stale Data on LLMs?\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Haizhong Zheng , cite7†Jiawei Zhao , cite8†Bedi Chen L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Prosperity before Collapse: How Far Can Off-Policy RL Reach with Stale Data on LLMs?, by Haizhong Zheng and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Reinforcement learning has been central to recent advances in large language model reasoning, but most algorithms rely on on-policy training that demands fresh rollouts at every update, limiting efficiency and scalability. Asynchronous RL systems alleviate this by decoupling rollout generation from training, yet their effectiveness hinges on tolerating large staleness in rollout data, a setting where existing methods either degrade in performance or collapse.\nL17: We revisit this challenge and uncover a prosperity-before-collapse phenomenon: stale data can be as informative as on-policy data if exploited properly. Building on this insight, we introduce M2PO (Second-Moment Trust Policy Optimization), which constrains the second moment of importance weights to suppress only extreme outliers while preserving informative updates.\nL18: Notably, M2PO sharply reduces the fraction of clipped tokens under high staleness (from 1.22% to 0.06% over training), precisely masking high-variance tokens while maintaining stable optimization. Extensive evaluation across six models (from 1.7B to 32B) and eight benchmarks shows that M2PO delivers stable off-policy training even with data stale by at least 256 model updates and matches on-policy performance.\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2510.01161 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2510.01161v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.01161†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Haizhong Zheng [cite14†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 1 Oct 2025 17:48:23 UTC (581 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Tue, 28 Oct 2025 03:28:48 UTC (581 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Prosperity before Collapse: How Far Can Off-Policy RL Reach with Stale Data on LLMs?, by Haizhong Zheng and 2 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite9†View PDF L36:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite15†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L45: \nL46: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite22†cs L51: cite23†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite29†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Citations (cite30†What are Smart Citation